WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
- From health care to
education to advertising,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.480
data is helping businesses
innovate to new heights.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.120
A key challenge is not
just how to find data,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.340
but how to make it useful.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.440
Data scientists
and data engineers

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.890
often need to build data
pipelines to ingest, process,

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.230
and analyze their data.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.680
These ETL and ELT
type workflows usually

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.300
involve multiple
services that need

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.940
to be coordinated and
executed at the right time

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.280
and under the right conditions.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.200
In our previous
video, we learned

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.740
about how orchestration is
the automation, management,

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.350
and coordination of workflows.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.360
In this video,
we'll focus on how

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.660
you can orchestrate your data
workflows in Google Cloud using

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:43.770
Cloud Composer.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.890
This is where Cloud Composer,
Google Cloud's fully managed

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.570
orchestration service,
comes into play.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:53.100
You can use Composer to
create, schedule, and monitor

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.040
end-to-end workflows
using Python.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.800
Composer is built on the
open source Apache Airflow

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.710
Project, which has a huge
community of contributors

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.250
and users.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.280
And since it's a
fully managed service,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.340
you don't have to worry about
managing Airflow deployments.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.380
Composer takes care of
infrastructure management

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.320
for you, so you can focus on
actually building your data

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.690
pipelines.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.090
Composer supports many
different use cases,

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.363
but most of its users
are data engineers

00:01:20.363 --> 00:01:22.530
who are building workflows
to orchestrate their data

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.210
pipelines.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.940
There are several key advantages
that Composer has for the data

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.410
workflows use case.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.590
First, the Airflow Project
has hundreds of operators

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.690
and sensors you can use.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.280
These make it easy
to communicate

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.900
across multiple
Cloud environments

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.840
with very little code.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.880
In addition to Google
Cloud operators,

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.610
there are multiple operators
for other common interfaces

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:46.010
like MySQL and Slack.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:49.520
Second, Composer has
sophisticated task management.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.250
You can parallelize and
branch tasks and define

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.390
how you want to handle failure.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.700
For example, you could send
out an email to the team

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.620
if something goes
wrong along the way.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.930
And third, keeping
track of your workflows

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.840
is straightforward with
Composer's built-in monitoring.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.240
All of this is important
as your workflows scale

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.910
and become more complex.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.180
And finally, Composer comes
with enterprise-ready security

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:17.500
features, like customer-managed
encryption keys and VPC service

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.290
control support,
enabling organizations

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:22.030
to use it right away
without worrying

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.430
about security compliance.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.590
It's important to note
that Composer is not

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.530
designed to transform,
process, or pass along

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.120
large amounts of data.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.330
Instead, Composer is great
for managing and coordinating

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:38.290
tasks delegated to other
products that handle your data.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.870
Some products you might want
to coordinate using Composer

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:45.820
include BigQuery, Dataflow,
Dataproc, and Data Fusion.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:48.970
These are all great at
operating directly on big data.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.740
Let's take a look
at an example use

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.500
case of how Composer can use
to orchestrate a big data

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:54.820
pipeline.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.940
Composer tasks are defined in
the form of a Python Directed

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.640
Acyclic Graph, or DAG.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.980
First, we monitor a
Cloud Storage bucket

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.190
and trigger the pipeline as
soon as the file appears.

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:09.570
Then we kick off a
Cloud Dataflow pipeline.

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:12.300
Next, we execute
BigQuery SQL job

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.650
to validate the process data.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.380
And finally, we
delete the object

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:19.660
from the Cloud Storage bucket
since we no longer need it.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.910
Along the way, if
anything goes wrong,

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.850
a Slack notification could
be sent to team members.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.410
And there you have it.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.050
Data orchestration is becoming
increasingly important

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:30.700
for data engineers
and developers

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.040
to manage their
tasks and services.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.220
By having Composer orchestrate
workflows on their behalf,

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.080
data engineers can
focus their efforts

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.600
on the more creative
aspects of data engineering.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.120
Make sure to check out the
next video in our series, where

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:46.130
we'll learn more about
how to build workflows

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.010
in Cloud Composer.

